<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">East African Community</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East African Community is an intergovernmental organisation of East African states with its headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arusha">Arusha</a>, Tanzania. A first community of Kenya, Tanzania and Uganda, formed in 1967, collapsed in 1977 amid political disputes; the same three states revived it by a treaty signed in 1999 that came into force in 2000. It has since admitted Rwanda, Burundi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalia">Somalia</a>, and established a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> and common market.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-african-community.jpg" alt="East African Community" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin23230 File:South Sudan (orthographic projection) highlighted.svg Spesh531 File:Africa-countries-EAC.svg CrazyPhunk · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:East_African_Community_(orthographic_projection).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
